New in 3.0.0 (2026-11-13):
- Rewrote FEPX completely in modern Fortran (2018) and MPI, with the same
  elasto-viscoplastic crystal-plasticity formulation (finite strains,
  anisotropic elasticity, rate-dependent slip). Inputs of FEPX 2.x must
  be ported: the configuration file is new and its parser is strict
  (every error is fatal and gives the file and line).
- Added Kocks-Mecking dislocation-density hardening, with optional
  irradiation softening.
- Reorganized the saturation hardening models: isotropic or anisotropic
  (with a full interaction matrix), each with optional saturation
  evolution, cyclic hardening and precipitate strengthening.
- Added the full anisotropic elastic stiffness of hcp crystals (c33).
- Added 20-node hexahedral elements (with 10-node tetrahedral elements).
- Unified the loading: boundary-condition presets, general velocity
  conditions, multi-point constraints and periodic conditions, with
  per-step strain, time, load and stress targets and strain, load and
  stress rates (with dwells).
- Added HDF5 output with an XDMF file for ParaView, alongside the
  simulation.sim directory read by Neper.
- Added a nodal block-Jacobi preconditioner (the default) to the
  conjugate gradient solver.
- Added an elastic initialization step (initialization elastic_step, the
  default). The isotropic viscoplastic start, the default of FEPX 2.x,
  remains available (initialization isotropic_viscoplastic).
- Made results independent of the number of MPI processes, and added
  unit, verification and regression tests (ctest).
- Fixed many errors of FEPX 2.x (e.g. the last slip system never hardened
  under isotropic hardening).
- Added conda packages (an Open MPI and an MPICH build, with the HDF5
  output), built and tested by make conda.
- Rewrote the manual, with new tutorials; switched to the MIT license.
- Added the version history to the documentation (Versions), and aligned
  the documentation with Neper's (heading levels and case, name,
  reference papers as citations).

